    {
      "id": "scene-02",
      "title": "Complex Numbers in Polar Form",
      "durationSeconds": 25,
      "startFrame": 601,
      "endFrame": 1350,
      "narration": {
        "speaker": "QUALUTION Narrator",
        "text": "Any complex amplitude \u03b1 can be written in rectangular form as a + bi, or in polar form as r times e to the power of i theta. Here, r is the magnitude, and theta is the phase angle.",
        "tone": "Authoritative, pedagogically clear, engaging",
        "pacingWpm": 130
      },
      "captions": [
        {
          "text": "Rectangular form: \u03b1 = a + bi.  Polar form: \u03b1 = r e^(i\u03b8).",
          "startFrame": 601,
          "endFrame": 960
        },
        {
          "text": "r represents the amplitude magnitude; \u03b8 represents the phase angle.",
          "startFrame": 961,
          "endFrame": 1350
        }
      ],
      "onScreenText": [
        {
          "id": "txt_polar",
          "text": "\u03b1 = a + bi = r \u00b7 e^(i\u03b8)",
          "role": "formula",
          "position": [
            0.0,
            1.2,
            0.1
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "txt_mag",
          "text": "Magnitude: r = \u221a(a\u00b2 + b\u00b2)  |  Phase: \u03b8 = arctan(b/a)",
          "role": "body",
          "position": [
            0.0,
            -0.6,
            0.1
          ]
        }
      ],
      "visualElements": [
        {
          "id": "vis_2_bg",
          "type": "background_plate",
          "source": "assets/backgrounds/deep_space_grid.png",
          "layer": "background"
        },
        {
          "id": "vis_2_stage",
          "type": "vector_diagram",
          "source": "procedural_blender_object",
          "layer": "primary"
        }
      ],
      "animationActions": [
        {
          "target": "vis_2_stage",
          "action": "fade_in_and_settle",
          "startFrame": 601,
          "durationFrames": 30
        }
      ],
      "assetsRequired": [
        "deep_space_grid.png",
        "ibm_quantum_palette.blend"
      ],
      "educationalPurpose": "Review Euler's formula and polar decomposition of complex amplitudes.",
      "transitionOut": "smooth_crossfade"
    },
    {
      "id": "scene-03",
      "title": "Why Negative Amplitudes Matter",
      "durationSeconds": 30,
      "startFrame": 1351,
      "endFrame": 2250,
      "narration": {
        "speaker": "QUALUTION Narrator",
        "text": "Consider the state |+\u27e9 with amplitude +1/\u221a2, and |-\u27e9 with amplitude -1/\u221a2 for state |1\u27e9. Both square to 1/2 upon measurement. But before measurement, adding +1/\u221a2 and -1/\u221a2 results in zero\u2014complete cancellation!",
        "tone": "Authoritative, pedagogically clear, engaging",
        "pacingWpm": 130
      },
      "captions": [
        {
          "text": "|+1/\u221a2|\u00b2 = 1/2, and |-1/\u221a2|\u00b2 = 1/2. Measurement probabilities are identical.",
          "startFrame": 1351,
          "endFrame": 1770
        },
        {
          "text": "Yet amplitudes can add constructively (1) or destructively cancel to 0!",
          "startFrame": 1771,
          "endFrame": 2250
        }
      ],
      "onScreenText": [
        {
          "id": "txt_cancel",
          "text": "Destructive Amplitude Interference",
          "role": "header",
          "position": [
            0.0,
            2.0,
            0.1
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "txt_math",
          "text": "(+1/\u221a2) + (-1/\u221a2) = 0",
          "role": "formula",
          "position": [
            0.0,
            0.2,
            0.1
          ]
        }
      ],
      "visualElements": [
        {
          "id": "vis_3_bg",
          "type": "background_plate",
          "source": "assets/backgrounds/deep_space_grid.png",
          "layer": "background"
        },
        {
          "id": "vis_3_stage",
          "type": "vector_diagram",
          "source": "procedural_blender_object",
          "layer": "primary"
        }
      ],
      "animationActions": [
        {
          "target": "vis_3_stage",
          "action": "fade_in_and_settle",
          "startFrame": 1351,
          "durationFrames": 30
        }
      ],
      "assetsRequired": [
        "deep_space_grid.png",
        "ibm_quantum_palette.blend"
      ],
      "educationalPurpose": "Demonstrate destructive cancellation mathematically.",
      "transitionOut": "smooth_crossfade"
    },
    {
      "id": "scene-04",
      "title": "Global Phase Invariance",
      "durationSeconds": 30,
      "startFrame": 2251,
      "endFrame": 3150,
      "narration": {
        "speaker": "QUALUTION Narrator",
        "text": "What happens if we multiply the entire quantum state by a phase factor e to the i gamma? When we calculate measurement probabilities, the phase factor conjugates to one and vanishes. Global phase is physically undetectable!",
        "tone": "Authoritative, pedagogically clear, engaging",
        "pacingWpm": 130
      },
      "captions": [
        {
          "text": "Multiplying a state by e^(i\u03b3): |\u03c8'\u27e9 = e^(i\u03b3)|\u03c8\u27e9.",
          "startFrame": 2251,
          "endFrame": 2640
        },
        {
          "text": "Probability: |e^(i\u03b3)\u03c8|\u00b2 = |e^(i\u03b3)|\u00b2 |\u03c8|\u00b2 = 1 \u00b7 |\u03c8|\u00b2. Global phase is undetectable!",
          "startFrame": 2641,
          "endFrame": 3150
        }
      ],
      "onScreenText": [
        {
          "id": "txt_global",
          "text": "Global Phase Invariance: |e^(i\u03b3)\u03c8|\u00b2 = |\u03c8|\u00b2",
          "role": "formula",
          "position": [
            0.0,
            0.8,
            0.1
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "txt_note",
          "text": "Global phase leaves all physical observables unchanged",
          "role": "subheader",
          "position": [
            0.0,
            -0.8,
            0.1
          ]
        }
      ],
      "visualElements": [
        {
          "id": "vis_4_bg",
          "type": "background_plate",
          "source": "assets/backgrounds/deep_space_grid.png",
          "layer": "background"
        },
        {
          "id": "vis_4_stage",
          "type": "vector_diagram",
          "source": "procedural_blender_object",
          "layer": "primary"
        }
      ],
      "animationActions": [
        {
          "target": "vis_4_stage",
          "action": "fade_in_and_settle",
          "startFrame": 2251,
          "durationFrames": 30
        }
      ],
      "assetsRequired": [
        "deep_space_grid.png",
        "ibm_quantum_palette.blend"
      ],
      "educationalPurpose": "Formalize the proof of global phase invariance.",
      "transitionOut": "smooth_crossfade"
    },
    {
      "id": "scene-05",
      "title": "The Power of Relative Phase",
      "durationSeconds": 35,
      "startFrame": 3151,
      "endFrame": 4200,
      "narration": {
        "speaker": "QUALUTION Narrator",
        "text": "In contrast, the relative phase\u2014the difference in phase between |0\u27e9 and |1\u27e9\u2014is intensely physical! By factoring out global phase, any single-qubit pure state can be written using two angles: theta and phi.",
        "tone": "Authoritative, pedagogically clear, engaging",
        "pacingWpm": 130
      },
      "captions": [
        {
          "text": "Relative phase is the phase angle difference between |0\u27e9 and |1\u27e9.",
          "startFrame": 3151,
          "endFrame": 3600
        },
        {
          "text": "Canonical parameterization: |\u03c8\u27e9 = cos(\u03b8/2)|0\u27e9 + e^(i\u03c6)sin(\u03b8/2)|1\u27e9.",
          "startFrame": 3601,
          "endFrame": 4200
        }
      ],
      "onScreenText": [
        {
          "id": "txt_param",
          "text": "|\u03c8\u27e9 = cos(\u03b8/2)|0\u27e9 + e^(i\u03c6) sin(\u03b8/2)|1\u27e9",
          "role": "formula",
          "position": [
            0.0,
            0.5,
            0.1
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "txt_angles",
          "text": "\u03b8 \u2208 [0, \u03c0] (Colatitude)  |  \u03c6 \u2208 [0, 2\u03c0) (Longitude)",
          "role": "subheader",
          "position": [
            0.0,
            -1.0,
            0.1
          ]
        }
      ],
      "visualElements": [
        {
          "id": "vis_5_bg",
          "type": "background_plate",
          "source": "assets/backgrounds/deep_space_grid.png",
          "layer": "background"
        },
        {
          "id": "vis_5_stage",
          "type": "vector_diagram",
          "source": "procedural_blender_object",
          "layer": "primary"
        }
      ],
      "animationActions": [
        {
          "target": "vis_5_stage",
          "action": "fade_in_and_settle",
          "startFrame": 3151,
          "durationFrames": 30
        }
      ],
      "assetsRequired": [
        "deep_space_grid.png",
        "ibm_quantum_palette.blend"
      ],
      "educationalPurpose": "Derive the standard spherical parameterization of a single qubit.",
      "transitionOut": "smooth_crossfade"
    },
    {
      "id": "scene-06",
      "title": "Phase on the Bloch Equator",
      "durationSeconds": 35,
      "startFrame": 4201,
      "endFrame": 5250,
      "narration": {
        "speaker": "QUALUTION Narrator",
        "text": "When theta equals 90 degrees, the qubit sits on the equator of the Bloch sphere. Here, varying the relative phase phi rotates the state from |+\u27e9 at phi equals 0, to |i\u27e9 at 90 degrees, to |-\u27e9 at 180 degrees.",
        "tone": "Authoritative, pedagogically clear, engaging",
        "pacingWpm": 130
      },
      "captions": [
        {
          "text": "On the equator (\u03b8 = \u03c0/2), varying relative phase \u03c6 rotates the vector.",
          "startFrame": 4201,
          "endFrame": 4680
        },
        {
          "text": "\u03c6 = 0 gives |+\u27e9; \u03c6 = \u03c0/2 gives |+i\u27e9; \u03c6 = \u03c0 gives |-\u27e9.",
          "startFrame": 4681,
          "endFrame": 5250
        }
      ],
      "onScreenText": [
        {
          "id": "txt_eq",
          "text": "Equatorial Relative Phase States",
          "role": "header",
          "position": [
            0.0,
            2.5,
            0.1
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "txt_p_state",
          "text": "|+\u27e9 = (|0\u27e9 + |1\u27e9)/\u221a2 (\u03c6 = 0)",
          "role": "body",
          "position": [
            0.0,
            1.0,
            0.1
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "txt_i_state",
          "text": "|+i\u27e9 = (|0\u27e9 + i|1\u27e9)/\u221a2 (\u03c6 = \u03c0/2)",
          "role": "body",
          "position": [
            0.0,
            0.0,
            0.1
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "txt_m_state",
          "text": "|-\u27e9 = (|0\u27e9 - |1\u27e9)/\u221a2 (\u03c6 = \u03c0)",
          "role": "body",
          "position": [
            0.0,
            -1.0,
            0.1
          ]
        }
      ],
